# Circles: Chords, Angles, and the Magic of π

> One number — π — links every circle's circumference to its
> diameter. Around it stands a family of theorems about chords, arcs,
> tangents and angles that turn circles into the most elegant chapter
> of geometry.

## 1. Parts of a Circle

A **circle** is the set of all points at a fixed distance (the
**radius** r) from a centre.

| Part | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| Radius | segment from centre to any point of the circle |
| Chord | segment joining two points of the circle |
| Diameter | a chord through the centre — the longest chord, 2r |
| Arc | a piece of the circumference between two points |
| Sector | pie-slice region bounded by two radii and an arc |
| Segment | region between a chord and its arc |
| Secant | a line cutting the circle at two points |
| Tangent | a line touching the circle at exactly one point |

## 2. π: The Constant Ratio

Measure the circumference of any circle and divide by its diameter:
the answer is always the same irrational number, **π ≈ 3.14159**.
Because π is irrational, no fraction — not even 22/7 — equals it
exactly; those are merely convenient approximations.

@math label="eq:pi-def"
\pi = \frac{C}{d} \approx 3.14159
@/math

## 3. The Circle Theorems

| Theorem | Statement |
| --- | --- |
| Central angle | the angle at the centre is **twice** any angle at the circumference standing on the same arc |
| Same segment | angles in the same segment (standing on one chord) are equal |
| Semicircle | the angle in a semicircle is exactly **90°** |
| Cyclic quadrilateral | opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to **180°** |
| Equal chords | equal chords subtend equal arcs and equal angles at the centre |
| Perpendicular chord | the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord |

[example]
If an angle at the circumference standing on arc AB is 35°, the angle
AOB at the centre on the same arc is 70°. An angle inscribed in a
semicircle (arc = 180°) is 90° — the semicircle theorem is really the
central-angle theorem with centre angle 180°.
[/example]

## 4. Tangents

A **tangent** is perpendicular to the radius at the point of
contact — the single most-used tangent fact in exams. Two tangents
drawn from the same external point are **equal in length**, so the
triangle they form with the line to the centre is symmetric.

[info title="Alternate segment theorem"]
The angle between a tangent and a chord through the point of contact
equals the angle in the alternate segment — the angle the chord
subtends anywhere on the far arc. It quietly powers most "prove the
angles equal" tangent questions.
[/info]

## 5. Measuring the Circle

@math label="eq:circle-measure"
C = 2\pi r
\qquad\qquad
A = \pi r^2
@/math

[example]
For radius 7 cm (with π ≈ 22/7): the circumference is 2 × 22/7 × 7 =
44 cm, and the area is 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm². The **annulus** between
two concentric circles of radii 5 and 3 has area π(5² − 3²) = 16π ≈
50.3 square units — outer minus inner, no need for separate pies.
[/example]

## 6. Arcs and Sectors

An arc is a **fraction of the circumference** and a sector the same
fraction of the area — the fraction is θ/360 for a central angle θ.

@math label="eq:arc-sector"
\text{arc length} = \frac{\theta}{360} \times 2\pi r
\qquad
\text{sector area} = \frac{\theta}{360} \times \pi r^2
@/math

[example]
With r = 7 and θ = 90°: the arc is (90/360) × 2π × 7 = 14π/4 = 11 cm
(using π ≈ 22/7), and the sector area is (90/360) × π × 49 = 154/4 =
38.5 cm² — exactly one quarter of the full circle, as 90° suggests.
[/example]

## 7. Cyclic Quadrilaterals

A **cyclic quadrilateral** has all four vertices on one circle. Its
opposite angles sum to 180° — 110° faces 70° — and each **exterior
angle equals the interior opposite angle**. Ptolemy's theorem (the
product of the diagonals equals the sum of the products of opposite
sides) holds for these quadrilaterals and generalises the distance
formulas of trigonometry.

## 8. Common Exam Patterns

| Question type | First move |
| --- | --- |
| Find the angle at the centre | double the circumference angle (or halve) |
| Two angles standing on one chord | equal — same segment |
| Radius meets a chord at 90° | it bisects the chord; use right triangles |
| Two tangents from a point | equal lengths; line to centre bisects |
| Shaded region | sector minus triangle (or annulus pieces) |
| Prove a 90° angle | stand it in a semicircle, or use tangent ⊥ radius |

[note]
Area of a segment = sector area − triangle area. For a chord
subtending 90° at the centre, the triangle is a right isosceles
triangle with legs r, so the segment is πr²/4 − r²/2.
[/note]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Ask yourself | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Circumference at a glance? | C = 2πr |
| Area at a glance? | A = πr² |
| r = 7, θ = 90°: arc, sector? | 11 cm, 38.5 cm² |
| Angle at centre for 35° at circumference? | 70° |
| Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral? | sum to 180° |
| Tangent and radius meet at? | 90° |
| Two tangents from an external point? | equal lengths |
